.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt PASSGEN 1
.Os Passgen v@PROJECT_VERSION@
.Sh NAME
.Nm passgen
.Nd generate random sequences from a regex-like pattern
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl ejz
.Op Fl a Ar amount
.Op Fl P Ar name:pattern
.Op Fl r Ar random
.Op Fl S Ar steps
.Op Fl w Ar name:path
.Op Fl m Ar master Op Fl d Ar domain Op Fl t Ar token
.Ar pattern | Fl p Ar preset
.Nm
.Fl l
.Nm
.Fl h | v
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
generates random sequences, such as passphrases, from a pattern.
The pattern is written in a language which is similar to regular expressions,
so it can describe sequences that fit any password policy.
There are presets for some common kinds of sequences.
.Pp
It can read wordlists, to pick random words from them, or to generate words
which are random but pronounceable, using a Markov chain.
It can also compute the entropy of what it generates, which says how hard it
is to guess.
.Pp
By default, the sequences are generated from the secure randomness source of
the system.
In master passphrase mode, they are derived from a master passphrase instead,
so that the same inputs always result in the same sequence.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a , Fl -amount Ar amount
Generate this many sequences, one per line.
Has to be at least one, which is the default.
.It Fl c , Fl -config Ar path
Read this configuration file, instead of the usual ones, see
.Sx CONFIGURATION .
Can be given more than once, to read several, in order.
Unlike the usual ones, it has to exist.
To read none, use
.Pa /dev/null .
.It Fl e , Fl -entropy
Compute the entropy of every generated sequence, in bits.
This measures how much randomness went into the sequence: for an entropy of
64 bits, someone who knows the pattern needs up to 2^64 guesses to find it.
The entropy is printed to standard error before the sequence, or included in
the output with
.Fl -json .
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Show a summary of the options and exit.
.It Fl j , Fl -json
Output the sequences as a JSON array of objects, rather than as lines of text.
Every object has the sequence as
.Li output ,
and its entropy as
.Li entropy
if
.Fl -entropy
is given.
.It Fl l , Fl -list
List the presets with their patterns and exit.
This includes the ones defined in the configuration, and by options, except
for those whose names start with an underscore.
.It Fl p , Fl -preset Ar name
Use the pattern of the preset with this name, see
.Sx PRESETS .
Cannot be used together with a pattern.
.It Fl P , Fl -define-preset Ar name:pattern
Define a preset.
If there is a preset with this name already, it is replaced.
.It Fl r , Fl -random Ar random
Use another source of randomness, see
.Sx RANDOMNESS SOURCES .
.It Fl S , Fl -steps Ar steps
Limit how many steps generating may take.
Has to be at least one, the default is 1000000.
Every character of a set, run of literal characters, word and group that is
generated is a step, counting each repetition: so
.Ql [a-z]{20}
takes 20 steps.
Generating fails for patterns which take more, which keeps a short pattern
such as
.Ql (){999999999}
from running practically forever.
.It Fl v , Fl -version
Show the version and exit.
.It Fl w , Fl -wordlist Ar name:path
Load the wordlist at
.Ar path ,
which is a file with one word per line, and make it available to patterns as
.Ar name .
If there is a wordlist with this name already, it is replaced.
The file is only read when a pattern uses the wordlist.
.It Fl z , Fl -null
Separate the sequences by a NUL character instead of a newline.
.El
.Pp
The following options are for master passphrase mode, in which the sequences
are not random, but derived from a master passphrase.
The same master passphrase, domain, token and pattern always result in the
same sequence, so there is no need to store it anywhere.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl m , Fl -master Ar master
The master passphrase.
Giving this option is what enables master passphrase mode.
.It Fl d , Fl -domain Ar domain
What the sequence is for, typically the domain of a website.
Optional.
.It Fl t , Fl -token Ar token
Anything else which should result in a different sequence, such as the name of
an account when there is more than one for a domain, or a counter to change a
passphrase.
Optional.
.El
.Pp
See
.Sx MASTER PASSPHRASE MODE
for how the sequences are derived.
.Sh PATTERNS
A pattern describes what to generate.
It may contain any Unicode characters, and has to be valid UTF-8.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li abc
Literal: outputs
.Ql abc .
.It Li abc|def|ghi
Alternatives: outputs one of them, chosen at random.
An alternative can be made more likely by starting it with a number in curly
braces:
.Ql {3}abc|def
outputs
.Ql abc
three times out of four.
.It Li (abc|def)
Group: outputs what is inside of it.
Groups are used to limit how far alternatives extend, and to repeat or make
optional more than a single character.
They can be nested, up to 1024 deep.
.It Li [abc] , [a-z] , [a-zA-Z0-9_]
Set: outputs one of the characters in it, chosen at random.
It has to have at least one.
It can list single characters, and ranges of characters, which include every
character from the first to the last one by Unicode codepoint.
The last character of a range must not come before the first one.
To have a dash or a closing bracket in a set, write them as
.Ql \e-
and
.Ql \e] .
A character which is listed more than once (also as part of a range) is as many
times as likely to be chosen: in
.Li [aab] ,
.Ql a
is twice as likely as
.Ql b .
The order does not matter, so
.Li [ba]
and
.Li [ab]
generate the same.
.It Li a{8} , [a-z]{8,12} , (ab){3}
Repetition: outputs what comes before it the given number of times.
With two numbers, it is a random number of times between the first and the
second one, including both.
The second number must not be smaller than the first one.
.It Li a\&? , [a-z]\&? , (abc)\&?
Optional: what comes before it is either output or left out, at random.
.It Li \ew{name}
Word: outputs a random word from the wordlist
.Ar name .
.It Li \em{name}
Markov chain: outputs a random word, generated letter by letter such that it
resembles the words in the wordlist
.Ar name .
These words are pronounceable, but mostly not real words, and their length is
not fixed.
.It Li \em{name:length}
The same, with a Markov chain of the given length, from 1 to 8.
This is how many of the previous letters are taken into account when choosing
the next one, which is 3 without a length.
Longer chains generate words which are closer to those in the wordlist, but
have less entropy and need more memory.
A pattern can use chains of different lengths from the same wordlist.
.It Li \ep{name}
Preset: outputs what the pattern of the preset
.Ar name
generates, as if it were in parentheses, see
.Sx PRESETS .
.El
.Pp
It is an error if a pattern uses a wordlist which was not loaded, or which is
empty, or a preset which is not defined.
This is checked before anything is generated.
.Pp
The following escape sequences can be used:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li \e( , \e) , \e[ , \e] , \e{ , \e} , \e| , \e\&?
The character itself, without its meaning in a pattern.
These cannot be used inside of a set, except for the brackets.
.It Li \ea , \eb , \ee , \ef , \en , \er , \et , \ev
The control characters bell, backspace, escape, form feed, newline, carriage
return, tab and vertical tab.
.It Li \e\e
A backslash.
.It Li \eu{...}
The character with the given Unicode codepoint, as up to six hexadecimal
digits, for example
.Ql \eu{fc}
for
.Ql u
with a diaeresis.
A character which has a meaning in a pattern still has it when it is written
this way.
.El
.Pp
Any other character after a backslash is an error.
.Sh PRESETS
A preset is a pattern with a name.
It can be used on its own with
.Fl -preset ,
or in other patterns as
.Li \ep{name} ,
also in the patterns of other presets.
This way, patterns can be built from parts, for example in the configuration:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[presets]
_segment = '[a-zA-Z0-9]{6}'
mine = '(\ep{_segment}-){2}\ep{_segment}'
.Ed
.Pp
Presets whose names start with an underscore are meant to be parts of others,
and are not listed by
.Fl -list .
A preset cannot use itself, also not through other presets, and groups can
only be nested 1024 deep in all.
A preset is only parsed when it is used, so one which is not valid is not an
error until then.
.Pp
These presets are predefined, more can be defined with
.Fl -define-preset
or in the configuration.
.Bl -tag -width "proquint128"
.It Li apple1
Four groups of three letters or digits, separated by dashes, such as
.Ql oKC-T37-Dew-Qyn .
.It Li apple2
Three groups of six letters or digits, separated by dashes, such as
.Ql mHXr4X-CiK4w6-hbjF7T .
.It Li edge
15 characters which are letters, digits, colons, underscores or dashes.
.It Li firefox
15 letters or digits, such as
.Ql aKTKyS9pPgAQ8Oz .
.It Li proquint16 , proquint32 , ... , proquint128
Pronounceable groups of five letters which alternate between consonants and
vowels, separated by dashes, such as
.Ql zabil-pukug-punud-lasup .
Every group has 16 bits of entropy, and the number is the entropy in total,
in steps of 16 up to 128.
.It Li uuid
A random (version 4) UUID, such as
.Ql 0072ca58-5966-497c-8657-a59fca93bf25 .
.El
.Sh RANDOMNESS SOURCES
By default, the secure random number generator of the system is used.
Other than master passphrase mode, that is what should be used to generate
anything that is meant to be secret.
Other sources can be chosen with
.Fl -random ,
mostly for testing.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Li system
The random number generator of the system, which is the default.
This is
.Xr getrandom 2
on Linux and
.Xr arc4random_buf 3
on macOS.
On other systems, it reads from
.Pa /dev/urandom .
.It Li file\&: Ns Ar path
Read random data from a file.
It has to supply as much data as is needed:
.Nm
aborts when it runs out.
.It Li chacha20-argon2\&: Ns Ar master Ns Op : Ns Ar domain Ns Op : Ns Ar token
What master passphrase mode uses, see
.Sx MASTER PASSPHRASE MODE .
Every part can be up to 128 bytes long, and cannot contain colons.
.It Li chacha20\&: Ns Ar key Ns Op : Ns Ar iv
The ChaCha20 stream cipher, with a key of 32 bytes and an initialization
vector of 8 bytes, written in hex: exactly 64 hex digits for the key, and 16
for the initialization vector.
Without one, the initialization vector is
.Ql passgen
followed by a zero byte.
This is deterministic, but the key is not derived from a passphrase, so this
is not a substitute for master passphrase mode.
.It Li xorshift\&: Ns Ar seed
The xorshift pseudorandom number generator, with a seed between 1 and 2^64-1.
This is deterministic, and not secure.
.It Li zero
Only zeroes.
This is deterministic, and not random at all.
.El
.Sh MASTER PASSPHRASE MODE
In master passphrase mode, a key is derived from the master passphrase, the
domain and the token.
The key is used to generate a stream of pseudorandom data, which takes the
place of the randomness of the system.
.Pp
The key is derived with Argon2id, using 19 MiB of memory, 2 passes and 1 lane.
The master passphrase is the password, the salt is the fixed string
.Ql passgen/argon2id ,
the domain is the secret key and the token is the associated data.
The 32 byte result is the key for the ChaCha20 stream cipher (the original
variant, with a 64 bit counter starting at zero), with the initialization
vector
.Ql passgen
followed by a NUL byte.
.Pp
What is generated depends on all of the master passphrase, the domain, the
token and the pattern.
When the pattern uses a wordlist, it also depends on the contents of the
wordlist.
.Pp
Master passphrase mode cannot be used together with
.Fl -random ,
as it is a source of randomness itself.
.Pp
Before version 1.0.0, what is generated for the same inputs may change from
one version of
.Nm
to the next.
Changes to it are listed in the changelog.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
On startup,
.Nm
reads configuration files, which define presets and wordlists.
These are the ones listed in
.Sx FILES
which exist, in that order, unless others are given with
.Fl -config
or
.Ev PASSGEN_CONFIG .
Each can replace or remove what the ones before it define, and options on
the command line come last.
The format of these files is not stable yet, and may change in a future
version.
.Pp
They are written in TOML, for example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[wordlists]
english = "/usr/share/dict/words"
eff = "eff-large.txt"

[presets]
_segment = '[a-zA-Z0-9]{6}'
mine = '(\ep{_segment}-){2}\ep{_segment}'
apple1 = false
.Ed
.Pp
The table
.Li wordlists
defines wordlists by their paths, like
.Fl -wordlist .
A relative path is relative to the directory of the configuration file.
The table
.Li presets
defines presets, like
.Fl -define-preset .
Their patterns are best written in single quotes, where a backslash is just a
backslash.
Setting a preset or a wordlist to
.Li false
removes it, also a predefined one.
.Pp
The source of randomness cannot be set in the configuration, so that it is
always visible on the command line.
Anything else in a configuration file, and a file which is not valid TOML, is
an error.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev PASSGEN_CONFIG
Configuration files to read instead of the ones in
.Sx FILES ,
separated by colons.
Those which do not exist are skipped.
When it is set but empty, none are read.
.It Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where to look for the configuration of the user.
Defaults to
.Pa ~/.config .
.It Ev HOME
The home directory of the user.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa @CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX@/share/passgen/config.toml
Configuration which is installed along with
.Nm ,
which defines the wordlists installed next to it.
.It Pa @CMAKE_INSTALL_FULL_SYSCONFDIR@/passgen/config.toml
Configuration for all users.
.It Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/passgen/config.toml , ~/.config/passgen/config.toml
Configuration of the user.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success.
When there is an error, a message is printed to standard error, and it exits
with one of the following.
These may change between versions.
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 1
More than one pattern was given, or a pattern together with a preset.
.It 2
The source of randomness is not valid, or could not be opened.
.It 3
The pattern is not valid.
The message shows where in the pattern the error is.
.It 4
Out of memory.
.It 5
The amount is not valid.
.It 7
An option was not recognized.
If the pattern starts with a dash, put
.Ql --
before it.
.It 8
There is no preset with this name, or the definition of a preset is not
valid.
.It 9
Generating failed, for example because the pattern uses a wordlist which was
not defined, cannot be read, or is empty, or because it takes more steps than the step limit.
The message says which.
.It 10
The definition of a wordlist is not valid.
.It 12
The options for master passphrase mode cannot be used this way: together with
.Fl -random ,
or a domain or token without a master passphrase.
.It 13
The number of steps is not valid.
.It 14
A configuration file cannot be read, or is not valid.
.El
.Pp
If a source of randomness does not supply enough data,
.Nm
aborts.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Generate a passphrase from a preset:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -p apple1
fnK-Nvo-Ipj-W3M
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a passphrase of 16 random lowercase letters:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen '[a-z]{16}'
xqtrisxewuocrbrx
.Ed
.Pp
Generate five passphrases of twelve lowercase letters or digits:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -a 5 '[a-z0-9]{12}'
k8taknzsj324
lywtyf0dw6r9
qayn8jveduri
ddtoolrwmlk3
541kdz29gfdi
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a passphrase of 16 letters, digits and symbols, and compute its
entropy:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -e '[a-zA-Z0-9@#$()_=+]{16}'
entropy: 98.07 bits
9oqosbO@iEqH=nT6
.Ed
.Pp
The same as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -j -e '[a-zA-Z0-9@#$()_=+]{16}'
[{"output":"R(Jk7w3=yT_oGq#d","entropy":98.068528}]
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a passphrase of five random words, separated by dashes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -w english:/usr/share/dict/words '\ew{english}(-\ew{english}){4}'
nocturnal-evident-latter-is-five
.Ed
.Pp
Generate a passphrase of five pronounceable words from a Markov chain.
Compared to words from the wordlist, this has more entropy for the same
length, while still being memorable.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -w english:/usr/share/dict/words '\em{english}(-\em{english}){4}'
vuvulvers-Bachmer-rebacks-perventercizes-exually
.Ed
.Pp
Define a preset, and use it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -P 'pin:[0-9]{6}' -p pin
402918
.Ed
.Pp
Derive a passphrase for an account from a master passphrase.
This always results in the same passphrase:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ passgen -m mymasterpass -d example.com -t username -p apple2
6xnSzW-8Ptrv0-dngZFU
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr apg 1 ,
.Xr pwgen 1
.Pp
.Lk https://passgen.io "The website of Passgen" ,
which has more documentation and examples.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Patrick M. Elsen Aq Mt pelsen@xfbs.net
.Sh CAVEATS
Master passphrase mode is not stable before version 1.0.0: another version of
.Nm
may generate something else for the same inputs.
Keep a copy of the passphrases, or of the version which generated them.
.Pp
The master passphrase is given as an argument on the command line.
While
.Nm
is running, other users of the system may be able to see it in the list of
processes, and it may be saved in the history of the shell.
.Pp
What patterns which use Markov chains generate may change between versions of
.Nm ,
so they should not be relied on in master passphrase mode.
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
Deterministic sources of randomness other than master passphrase mode
.Po
.Li xorshift ,
.Li zero
and
.Li chacha20
with a key that is not secret
.Pc
must not be used to generate anything that is meant to be secret.
.Pp
The strength of everything derived in master passphrase mode is limited by
the strength of the master passphrase.
Someone who gets hold of one derived passphrase can try to guess the master
passphrase from it, and with it, all of the others.
